  <title>Write Linear Functions from a Graph: Mini-Lesson Review</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01219</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Write Linear Functions from a Graph: Mini-Lesson Review</md:title>
    <md:uuid>43a7a39d-54f6-4240-874a-fe4bce192030</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Write Linear Functions from a Graph</title>
<para>You can determine the slope and intercept of a line if the equation is written in slope-intercept form, \(y = mx + b\). You can also do the reverse—identify the slope and \(y\)-intercept and use them to find the equation of the line.</para>
<para>Write the linear function shown in the graph.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="The graph shows the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y-axes each run from negative 7 to 7. A line intercepts the \(y\)-axis at (0, negative 4), passes through the plotted point (3, negative 2), and intercepts the \(x\)-axis at (4, 0)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1543e8a34af5c80b92d0686f281c3f459054c314"/></media></figure></para>
<para>To find the slope, choose two points on the graph. The \(y\)-intercept is \((0, -4)\), and the graph passes through \((3, -2)\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Find the slope by counting the rise and run.</para>
<para>  \(m = \frac {rise}{run}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> \(m = \frac23\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Find the \(y\)-intercept.</para>
<para>  \(y\)-intercept \((0,-4)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Substitute the values into \(y=mx+b\).</para>
<para>  \(y=mx+b\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> \(y = \frac{2}{3}x=4\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6 -
  </emphasis>Rewrite the equation using function notation. </para>
<para>  \(f(x) = \frac{2}{3}x - 4\)</para>

<title>Try It: Write Linear Functions from a Graph</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Write the linear function shown in the graph.</para>
    <para><figure><media alt="The graph shows the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y-axes each run from negative 9 to 9. The point (3, negative 1) is plotted. A line intercepts the \(y\)-axis at (0, 2), intercepts the \(x\)-axis at (9 fourths, 0), and passes through the point (3, negative 1)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ea9eda36e31924bee294db2db4d403020d19c00d"/></media></figure></para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>Here is how to write a linear function from a graph.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Choose two points from the graph.</para>
  <para>    \((0, 3)\) and \((3, -1)\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Find the slope by counting the rise and run.</para>
  <para>    \(m=\frac{rise}{run}\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> \(m=-\frac43\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Find the \(y\)-intercept.</para>
  <para>    \((0, 3)\)</para>
  <para>Step 5 - 
  Substitute the values into \(y = mx + b\).</para>
  <para>    \(y = mx + b\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 6 - </emphasis></para>
  <para>    \(y=-\frac{4}{3}x+3\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 7 -</emphasis> Rewrite the equation using function notation.</para>
  <para>    \(f(x)=-\frac{4}{3}x+3\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Check Your Understanding</title><exercise data-content-id="d7bebde0-c39c-4cbb-8bc8-e287086de9df"><problem><para>
      <para dir="ltr">Which linear function could represent the graph?</para>
      <para dir="ltr"><figure><media alt="The graph shows the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y-axes each run from negative 7 to 7. A line intercepts the \(x\)-axis at (negative 2, 0), intercepts the \(y\)-axis at (0, 1) and passes through the plotted point (5, 4)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9e0202ee5be427cba55e81327bda2b33ebe3f319"/></media></figure></para>
    </para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(f(x)=\frac{3}{5}x+1\)</para><para>\(f(x)=\frac{5}{3}x+1\)</para><para>\(f(x)=-\frac{3}{5} x+1\)</para><para>\(f(x)=\frac{3}{5}x-\frac{5}{3}\)</para></problem><solution><para>\(f(x)=\frac{3}{5}x+1\)</para><para>
      <para>Try again. Let’s try again a different way: Notice that the \(y\)-intercept is \((0, 1)\). Use the points \((5, 4)\) and \((0, 1)\) to find the slope.</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section>

